title: The metadata headers
pid: 4434565e-a44d-4499-97e2-8faf88086921
body: |2-
  Most org headlines in the client will have a 'metadata header',
  consisting of some bare values and some key-value pairs
  separated by whitespace. Each kv pair is wrapped in parens: (key value).
  The whole collection is wrapped in (skg ...).
  This metadata appears right after the org bullet and a whitespace,
  and right before another whitespace and the node's title.

  The metadata grammar (which keys are recognized and how they're
  parsed) lives in =server/serve/parse_metadata_sexp.rs=. The
  in-memory types those keys correspond to are in
  =server/types/viewnode.rs= (=Viewnode=, =ViewnodeKind=, =Vognode=,
  =Folder=, =PropertyMember=, the diff view's =ChangeViewnode=, and its
  =ChangeFacts= and =ProcessedRequest=, which every =Viewnode= carries).

  Every headline has one shape:
  #+begin_example
  (skg (vnid X) [focused] [folded] [bodyFolded]
       KIND DECLARED...
       [(view FACT...)] [(diff FACT...)])
  #+end_example
  The parts are of two classes. The declared parts are what the client
  is responsible for and the save reads: the kind, the viewnode ID, the
  position atoms, =(gnid ID)=, =(skgRepos ...)=, =noClaim=,
  =writeProtected=, =contentInPart=, =(inPlaceOf ID)=, =(viewRequests
  ...)= and =(editRequest ...)=. The derived facts are what the server computes
  at every rendering and nobody reads back: everything in =(view ...)=
  (see "Stats metadata: the view facts" above) and in =(diff ...)=
  (see "Metadata of the diff view" below). The parser skips both forms
  whole. KIND is a bare atom, but five kinds carry their parameter in a
  small form headed by the kind: =(flag F)=, =(oldText ASPECT)=,
  =(homeSkgrepoMove ...)=, =(relRepoMove ...)= and =(changesetMarker
  KIND)=. A headline whose metadata names no kind is a node: a client
  writes =(skg folded)= on a headline that has no metadata yet. Each kind
  takes only its own declared parts:
  #+begin_example
  node [(gnid ID)] [(skgRepos [(nodeRepo R)] [(relRepo R)])]
       [noClaim] [writeProtected] [contentInPart] [(inPlaceOf ID)]
       [(editRequest [REQUEST] [PROCESSED])] [(viewRequests ...)]
       [(view ...)] [(diff ...)]
  ghost (gnid ID) (skgRepos (nodeRepo R)) [noClaim]
        [(editRequest [restoreNode] [PROCESSED])]
        [(view ...)] [(diff ...)]
  unknown (gnid ID) [(skgRepos (relRepo R))] [noClaim]
          [(editRequest [(relRepo R)] [PROCESSED])] [(diff ...)]
  restricted (gnid ID) [(skgRepos (relRepo R))] [noClaim]
             [(editRequest [(relRepo R)] [PROCESSED])] [(diff ...)]
  alias [(skgRepos (relRepo R))] [noClaim]
        [(editRequest [(relRepo R)] [PROCESSED])] [(view ...)] [(diff ...)]
  id [noClaim] [(editRequest PROCESSED)] [(view ...)] [(diff ...)]
  (flag F) [(editRequest PROCESSED)] [(view ...)] [(diff ...)]
  (oldText ASPECT), (homeSkgrepoMove ...), (relRepoMove ...),
  (changesetMarker KIND): [(editRequest PROCESSED)] [(diff ...)]
  bodyDiff, tombstone, forestRoot, a folder atom
  #+end_example
  where PROCESSED is =(processed OBJECT...)= or =(unprocessed OBJECT...)= (see
  "Metadata of the diff view"). =(gnid ID)= names the node a node, a
  ghost, an unknown viewnode or a restricted viewnode (=restricted=, see
  "Restricted-skgrepo nodes") stands for, and =(skgRepos (nodeRepo REPO)
  [(relRepo REPO)])= its home skgrepo and the skgrepo recording its
  relationship to its viewparent, the relRepo written only when it
  differs from the default (a ghost has no relRepo; an unknown viewnode,
  a restricted viewnode and an alias have only one). A restricted viewnode
  carries no title, and text typed on its line is refused, as on an
  unknown viewnode's. =noClaim= on a node, a ghost, an alias, an ID, an
  unknown viewnode or a restricted viewnode says the save does not read it as a member of its
  viewparent's list; a claiming child carries nothing, and so does every
  child of a viewparent whose claims the save does not read. A removed
  alias and a dangling partner the worktree lost (an unknown viewnode carrying
  =(rel removed)=) carry =noClaim= where their viewparent's claims are
  read, so that the save, which skips the =(diff ...)= saying they were
  lost, leaves them out of the list.
  =(editRequest ...)= holds at most two requests: one edit request and
  one processed request, written after it.

  Every viewnode's metadata starts with its viewnode ID, =(vnid X)=, where
  X is one or more lowercase letters and digits: =(skg (vnid 0a) node
  (gnid 1) (skgRepos (nodeRepo main)))=. It identifies the viewnode within its buffer for the
  life of the buffer. The server assigns one, from the view's counter in
  base 36 (two characters at least, skipping any spelled like a number,
  such as =05= or =1e5=), to every viewnode that lacks one at the end of a
  refresh, and while it holds the view never reassigns or reuses one: a headline keeps the
  viewnode ID it carried through a save and a rerender, through a cut
  and paste, and through a switch of the skgrepo restriction that leaves it standing. A
  headline typed without one gets one; of two headlines carrying one
  viewnode ID, the first in buffer order keeps it and the second (a copy)
  gets a fresh one. What else a saved headline keeps depends on whether
  the server holds the view the save names (it holds a view per
  connection, from the view's opening or a save of it until the view is
  closed or the connection ends). For a view it holds, a headline carrying one the
  view does not know (typed by hand, or brought back by an old copy of
  the buffer's text) gets a fresh one too, and the counter does not move
  past it. For a view it does not hold (a save on a new connection, or
  after a server restart), the buffer's own viewnode IDs are the view's:
  every headline keeps the one it carries, and the counter starts past
  the highest of them, so a fresh one is never one the buffer carries
  (though it can be one a headline removed before the save carried).
  Such a buffer may carry none past =1y2p0ij32e8e7= (half the counter's
  range, 'LARGEST_CARRIED_VIEWNODE_ID_COUNTER'), which leaves the
  counter room: a save of one carrying a larger ID (one typed by hand)
  is refused, naming it, and nothing is written. A buffer of the diff
  view is re-rendered as one only when the server holds it as one, or
  the save names its buffer ("Save buffer", =diff-view-buffer=).
  The clients show nothing for it and save it back untouched. The view's
  record of its viewnode IDs, with the counter and the viewnode IDs of
  the viewforest as last rendered, is 'ViewnodeIdRecord' in
  =server/types/views_state.rs=.

  The parser refuses, with a message naming the grammar, the retired
  spellings: =(node ...)=, =(ghost ...)= and =(unknown ...)= as forms,
  =(deleted ...)=, =(historical ...)=, =(id ID)=, =(repo REPO)=, a
  top-level =(relRepo REPO)=, =(FOLDER changed)=, =(rels ...)=,
  =(viewStats ...)=, =omittedBody= or the old home skgrepo herald
  outside =(view ...)=, =(readyRequest ...)=, =(unreadyRequest ...)=,
  and the old atoms of a child claiming nothing and of a restricted
  node's retired placeholder ('parse_metadata_sexp.rs' names each).

  Metadata is not WYSIWYG; its appearance in the client
  is determined by =elisp/heralds-minor-mode.el=, whose rule table is
  served by Rust (see "Herald rules" above; the table lives in
  =server/heralds.rs=).

  Partner folders are represented in Rust as
  =ViewnodeKind::Folder (Folder)=, but their external metadata
  remains the established bare non-vognode atom:
  =forksFolder=,
  =hiderFolder=, =hiddenFolder=, =integratedElsewhereFolder=,
  =hiddenInForkPartnerFolder=, =hiddenByForkerFolder=,
  =hiddenOutsideOfForkPartnersFolder=, =containerFolder=, =mentionerFolder=,
  or, in the diff view only, =acquiredByFolder= or =acquiredFolder=;
  and, outside the diff view, =illegalEditFolder=.

  An =illegalEditFolder= stands first under an occurrence of a foreign
  node into which one of the user's skgrepos writes a section: an
  illegal edit to a foreign node ('IllegalEdit'; =docs/telescopes.org=,
  "Illegal edits"). The server draws it at every refresh while the
  graph holds such a section in a skgrepo the skgrepo restriction
  shows, and removes it otherwise. Its headline text is the section's
  title, its body the section's body (several sections' joined), and its
  children the section's =contains= members, write-protected. It is
  write-protected and save-inert: the parser keeps its text only for the
  refresh to replace, and a save reads nothing in it. Herald: =☮ ILLEGAL
  EDIT TO FOREIGN NODE=, in the message style.

  A tombstone is written =(skg (vnid X) tombstone)=: it has
  no ID, no change facts and no title (the parser discards text typed on
  its line), and holds only what stands below it. The refresh makes one
  of a headline that can stand where it is for nothing: an occurrence
  whose node a save deleted (in the diff view, its ghost instead, when
  HEAD has the node) or the skgrepo restriction keeps out where no
  visible record names it, a descriptor
  whose position its kind forbids, a change viewnode whose change is
  gone, an empty folder nothing can be added to, a ghost on request.
  The refresh prunes it once it is childless, a view root included, so a
  buffer may end with no headlines (the glossary's "tombstone,
  entombstone"). Herald "something stood here".
contains:
- 1f2d6cad-de82-4ed6-8425-6650515fea7d
- 31644a79-ec42-4a40-a005-1549c8ccb763
